{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.PhiBProd","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Summable"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor.phiBProd_ne_zero_off_lattice","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor.phiBProd_ne_zero_off_lattice","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PhiBProdDivisor.lean#L115-L123","doc":"**`phiBProd` is nonzero off the union of the two lattices.** Where every numerator and\ndenominator factor is nonzero, `phiBProd b z = (∏ num)/(∏ den) ≠ 0`: it has neither a zero\n(numerator nonvanishing) nor a pole (denominator nonvanishing) there. This is the\n`phiBProd`-side divisor computation the divisor-rigidity input consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor.tprod_denFactor_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor.tprod_denFactor_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PhiBProdDivisor.lean#L107-L113","doc":"**The denominator product has no spurious zeros** (hence `phiBProd` has no spurious\npoles). On the branch `Im b² > 0`, if every `denFactor b z k ≠ 0` (`z` off the dual\nlattice), then `∏' k, denFactor b z k ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor.tprod_numFactor_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdDivisor.tprod_numFactor_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PhiBProdDivisor.lean#L98-L105","doc":"**The numerator product has no spurious zeros.** On the branch `Im b² > 0`, if every\n`numFactor b z k ≠ 0` (i.e. `z` is off the `b`-zero-lattice), the whole product\n`∏' k, numFactor b z k ≠ 0`. So `phiBProd`'s numerator vanishes *exactly* where a factor\ndoes — no hidden zeros. ","signatureChunk":0}]}